// hw/p68.cst: physical constraints (pin locations and I/O standards) for the stand-alone CPU core builds made by
// tools/build.sh (top module p68_top). The file is deliberately tiny, and a beginner may wonder why a chip design
// needs so few pins: p68_top is only a timing test harness around the core, with one clock input, one serial input
// and one serial output. Its purpose is to give Gowin's place-and-route a complete design to place and time, so that
// the clock figure (Fmax) of the core can be measured. It is never loaded onto the board, so the pins were picked
// for convenience and are not wired to anything useful. The real board constraints belong to the full system
// (the A1200 around the core), which has its own file in its own repository.
//
// A .cst file is read by Gowin place-and-route. IO_LOC puts a port on a physical ball of the FPGA package (here the
// GW5AST-138C in the FPG676 package), and IO_PORT sets the electrical standard of that pin. Without IO_LOC the tool
// would choose pins itself, which is legal but makes the result differ from run to run. tools/build.sh copies this
// file into the build folder (as p.cst) and may append more lines to the copy, for example CLOCK_LOC lines that put
// a clock enable on a global clock line (see tools/build.sh).

// The clock input. The board has a 50 MHz oscillator on pin P16, but this timing-only file uses M21, a pin that was
// found to work for a clock entering the chip in these builds. LVCMOS33 is plain 3.3 V logic. PULL_MODE=NONE and DRIVE=OFF because this pin only ever receives a signal.
IO_LOC "clk" M21;
IO_PORT "clk" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 PULL_MODE=NONE DRIVE=OFF BANK_VCCIO=3.3;

// Serial input to the harness. PULL_MODE=UP holds the pin at logic 1 when nothing is connected, which is also the
// idle level of a serial line, so an unconnected input behaves like an idle line.
IO_LOC "din" K16;
IO_PORT "din" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 PULL_MODE=UP BANK_VCCIO=3.3;

// Serial output from the harness. DRIVE=8 is the output current in milliamps, a moderate value that is enough for a
// lightly loaded 3.3 V pin.
IO_LOC "dout" P15;
IO_PORT "dout" DRIVE=8 IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 BANK_VCCIO=3.3;
